  "question": {
    "text": "When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> potential difference is applied across a wire of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>, the drift speed of electrons is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2.5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mi>&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> . If the electron density in the wire is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>28</mn></msup><mi>&#160;</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math> , the resistivity of the material is close to:"
